WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.290
The job of a bartender is
not unlike that of a chef.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.210
He must learn all the flavours of spirits,
visualise them and create cocktails.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.980
-Very well done.
-Very well done.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.570
The day has finally come
for you to leave this place.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:02.580
Just what have you learned and what have
you gained at this bar counter, I wonder.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.540
We don't remember what
we are taught through words.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.290
Only through difficulty and hardship,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.960
by pouring one's heart and soul
into something do we ever truly learn.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.880
But these two gentlemen showed
great concern for me,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.050
without my ever knowing.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.550
The hardships and
difficulties, which you faced...

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.300
are the same as those
we ourselves had to overcome.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.850
-And so we are here, cheering you on.
-And so we are here, cheering you on.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.900
A young man sits alone at the counter
hanging his head low.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.200
He is a theatre troupe member
and loves acting.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.820
But his dream is way off in the distance
and tomorrow he goes back to his hometown.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.240
He goes to work in
a small backcountry office,

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.370
and lives with his overbearing parents.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.960
This cocktail is called the Red Eye.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.340
We don't normally use dark beer,

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.130
but I thought I would make it
a little more bitter this time.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.720
That sweetness and depth is a comforting
taste he had never enjoyed before.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.100
The sparkle bursting from within...

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.020
They remind him of his dreams.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.690
Vanishing as they burst.
Bursting as they vanish.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.480
Inside that never-ending,
forever ongoing sparkle,

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.190
he remembers the person he was once,
the person who once dreamed.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.530
Drinks served as pick-me-ups
or to welcome a customer.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.700
We call these reviver cocktails.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:30.040
"Reviver".
Meaning "to revitalise" or "re-energise".

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:36.420
All the spirits found in a bar
are reviver cocktails to its customers.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.590
When we lose our way,
or are burned out from work,

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.680
if we just push open the door,
we will be revived.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.470
Maybe that is why we have bars.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.730
All happy families are alike,

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.400
each unhappy family is
unhappy in its own way.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.980
That is the opening line from
Tolstoy's "Anna Karenina".

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.280
And this man here,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.240
is carrying one such unhappiness.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.490
All that he is now comes from the power
that he inherited from his father.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.080
His father left him
all his money and fame.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.830
But he used up all the talent, leaving
his son with nothing but the remains.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:17.670
That's what he thinks anyway.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.670
He calls himself "Marc".

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.010
Marc is a brandy made from
the remains of wine grapes,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.510
and the dregs left in the wine barrel.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.180
Marc in French, means "scum".

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.100
What's more, Marc
cannot be used in cocktails.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.520
Marc is best drunk on its own.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.940
That's why he calls himself "scum".

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.070
"Scum that has no other possible use."

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.530
It is like a stake that has pierced
his heart which he cannot pull out.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:51.950
But, even among scum,
there is the world's best scum.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.330
A sweet vermouth mixed with Marc.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.340
I have mixed Marc with Champagne.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.460
Or perhaps you might like
it mixed with orange juice?

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:09.350
You might notice it brings out unipue
flavours, compared to drinking it neat.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.600
Even scum stops being scum,
if you know how to bring out its best.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.230
Some customers are unable to convey
their feelings to bartenders

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.360
without being condescending, picking
fights, bragging or getting angry.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.740
When you're happy,
you only need a single cocktail.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.830
Whatever you drink tastes good.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.000
But if unhappiness
takes 100 different shapes,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.330
I choose to be a bartender

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.710
who can create 100 different cocktails
to ease that unhappiness.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.920
That is why cocktails come
in all different strengths,

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:51.890
colours, temperatures and drinking styles.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.890
A customer once said,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:59.310
"The God of Bars must be a woman."

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.980
"Goddesses have a little
jealousy in them."

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.570
"Which is why love found at the bar
vanishes like smoke, in the end."

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.070
A man and woman meet at a bar.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.580
They share a glass and spend
a quiet moment together.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.080
Each night they both take
a single cigar from the box.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.750
They promise to exchange affections once
all the cigars in the box are gone.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.750
And finally there were only two left...

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.680
Bartenders never cross the line by
getting involved in customers' affairs.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.230
But if we get a customer
smoking a cigar alone,

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.520
who was supposed
to be smoking it with a lady...

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.860
Bring me a drink fit
for the circumstances.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.190
...place a drinks order.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.990
There is a particular group
called "cigar cocktails"

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.570
that are meant to be
accompanied by a cigar.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.790
So that it isn't overpowered by the cigar
flavours, we use rum or cognac.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.210
Next some honey and
lastly cinnamon sticks.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.880
It has a rich and heavy flavour.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.640
The bartender from Bar Hemingway in Paris,

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.560
Johann Burgos,
came up with this original creation.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:20.600
It has "No Name",
which is in fact its name.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.440
Perhaps it is left nameless,
so that those drinking it

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.570
can give it whatever name
they feel matches their mood.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.450
Dry gin, dry sherry,

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.820
dry vermouth, Dubonnet,

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.950
15 ml of each.

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:42.160
And one dash of Grand Marnier.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:43.830
And stir.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.210
But the issue is with the stir itself.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.090
Dry vermouth is a flavoured wine that
retains the taste of white wine

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.220
and is aromatised with herbs.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.720
Sherry is a fortified wine from Spain
in which the alcohol is strengthened.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.810
And Dubonnet is a type of
wine served as an aperitif.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:08.150
It was once used in Peru as
a treatment against malaria.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:13.610
It is a red wine blended with quina
tree bark, then left to age in the barrel.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:17.660
The alcohol content is quite low,
and has a delicate flavour.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.040
Basically, it is delicious enough when
drunk neat,

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.250
so attempting to mix it
with anything is risky.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.960
And the name of the finished cocktail...

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.130
Bartender.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:36.340
I often think about why
this drink is called the Bartender.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:40.010
Does my technique make
the spirits taste delicious?

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.020
Does the flavour get conveyed
to the customer I wonder?

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:45.600
Or am I maybe doing
something that's unnecessary?

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.270
And in this drink...

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.440
No, in all drinks in bars,
what is found within...

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.320
is fear of the customer.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.410
I am continually in fear of the customer...

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.080
Which is why I forever strive to
achieve the perfect flavour combination.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.920
A bartender is no more than
a bar's secret ingredient.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:14.170
Why? Because the one who creates
the bar in the truest sense,

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.880
is the customer himself.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.430
Whether they are a regular,
or a first-time customer,

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:23.720
we create their drink with the thought
that it may be their first and last drink.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:27.020
Anybody can learn
how to appreciate a cocktail.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:28.850
And any cocktail has hidden inside it,

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.280
the magic to change
a customer's life just a little.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:37.320
Bars are clinics for the soul
which open only at night.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:41.240
People's spirits change. Which is why
we don't change the taste of spirits.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:45.790
That way, when customers come to the bar,
they meet their past selves.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:48.500
Drinks that connect people's hearts.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:51.790
It's those kinds of drinks
we prepare at bars.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:55.880
We dedicate our entire lives to
the formless world of service.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.880
The wish built into
the entrance of every bar...

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:04.520
We hope that when you leave, you are
a little happier than when you came in.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:13.270
In a bar, there may be only small
differences in the various glass shapes.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:20.860
But just those small differences can
make the drink taste totally different.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:27.250
Just what flavour is
this customer hoping to enjoy?

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.750
And so, what kind of spirit, drunk at
which temperature

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.710
and in which kind of glass would be best?

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.340
The bartender considers the motion of
his hand, even when spueezing a lime.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.310
One reason is because he wishes to make
the movements aesthetically pleasing.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:52.440
This is especially important when
the bar is full of people.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.440
Why, you ask?
Because until their drink arrives,

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:59.650
the customer spends their time
watching the bartender's hands.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:04.620
This time is another precious moment
spent at the bar.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.580
And what is more, all of the bartender's
skills and techniques

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:12.670
must exist for the customers.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.040
If one can make a cocktail quickly,

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:20.840
that much more time becomes
available to speak with the customer.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:24.850
Whether a bartender or not,

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:28.680
for those in the service industry,
when three misfortunes are accrued,

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.020
the heart begins to break.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:34.230
The first is a complaint from a customer.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.150
The next is a small mistake.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.150
And one more 'something or other'.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:44.490
You cannot call yourself a pro if
you cannot overcome these.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.790
The naturalist, Henry Thoreau, once said,

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:53.170
"I had three chairs in my house;"

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.960
"One for solitude,"

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.710
"two for friendship,"

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.470
"three for society."

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:05.590
Now, just what purpose does
the chair at the bar serve?

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:11.310
If you understand that, then surely you
won't be scared, no matter the customer.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:17.270
That is because chairs at a bar
are meant to soothe loneliness.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:24.240
Why does a bartender's training
begin with bottle polishing?

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:29.620
To dust and memorise the bar's
selection is, of course, one reason.

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:33.410
But also because a deep love for
the craftsman's history

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:37.080
and the culture that is locked
within each spirit

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:40.550
is revealed through that process.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:46.930
No matter the kind of customer,
there is no one who is "unsuited" to bars.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.680
If the customer feels uncomfortable

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.180
and does not like
the flavour of the spirits,

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:56.020
that is entirely
the fault of the bartender.

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.280
A sugar cube?

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:11.450
Yes, sir.

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.000
As you see,
a drop is infused into the sugar cube,

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.790
which is then allowed to dissolve
in one's mouth.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:21.630
I have heard of this.

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:26.430
If memory serves, it is a drinking method
passed down from the Chartreuse monastery.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:27.510
It is.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:31.810
I believe this may be where liqueurs
have their origins.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.020
A traveller went over the snowy Alps

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:38.650
and arrived at the Chartreuse monastery,
on the verge of collapse.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.270
The sweetness of the sugar was
to help his recovery from fatigue

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.610
and alcohol was added
for its reviving effects.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.570
Along with herbal remedies.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:51.280
The traveller regained his strength.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:58.420
The word Chartreuse carried the meaning of
a botanical miraculous medicine.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:03.800
And so, the traveller, whose life had been
saved, climbed down the mountain.

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:09.300
He did not know the name of
the abbot who had saved his life.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:15.060
But he could never forget
the intense flavour of the spirits

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.680
soaked into that sugar cube.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:18.560
Quite.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:22.020
That may be the same for this bar.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.070
Customers are surely like travellers.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.860
No matter how the spirits
saved their lives,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.490
they never remember the bartender.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:36.580
But they will never forget
the drink they enjoyed here.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.160
But then...

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.460
I think perhaps that is as it should be.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:43.960
As for why,

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:47.010
that is part of
the bartender's way of life.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:54.430
This bar keeps
a single bottle of Macallan.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:55.970
For generations,

00:14:56.060 --> 00:15:00.190
this bottle has been passed down to
every bartender who completes

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:01.770
their time at Eden hall.

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.360
I once passed it on to you.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:06.230
And tonight,

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:08.240
it is passed on to you.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.120
The Macallan, 1946.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.000
We call this special drink
"the water of life".

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:27.210
Its flavour is said to be the complete
opposite of the Macallan everyone knows.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:32.760
Unlike the original,
the flavour includes intense peat notes.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:38.180
That is because this bottle is dated 1946.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:43.770
To be precise, it is a bottle that
could only have been born that year.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.940
You can taste the special feelings
distilled in it by the whisky craftsmen.

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:56.280
That is indeed the reason why
this bottle was passed on to Eden hall.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:02.370
Peace returned to England
at the end of World War II.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:06.130
But with the battle wounds left behind,

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:11.050
along with all other commodities,
coal and oil energy was in short supply.

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:15.510
The struggle to find fuel to bake
daily bread was ever present.

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:20.560
However, from the peace that
had at last returned...

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:24.810
the snowmelt ran clear right before
the people's eyes, from the sun's rays.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.730
And next to it were fields of barley
stretching into the distance.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.480
And, seemingly ignorant of man's folly,

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:33.860
Mother Nature remained unchanged.

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:38.530
Surrounded by all this,
the whisky craftsmen all thought,

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:41.540
"I want to make whisky."

00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:46.790
But the coal which they needed to dry out
the barley was in shortage

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.540
and couldn't be obtained.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.000
And so they used peat instead.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.760
Only in the year 1946, they had no choice

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:59.800
but to use peat as fuel,
due to the coal shortage.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:03.140
It is because of this that The Macallan
ended up with a strong peat flavour

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:06.560
like whisky from Islay;
something it didn't originally have.

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:09.480
A different style to
the traditional Macallan.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.900
It was surely a risky gamble.

00:17:12.070 --> 00:17:16.780
But why did they take such
a risk to make this whisky?

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:23.410
Whisky maturation takes
as long as ten to twenty years.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:29.210
Over that period, the filled barrels
expand and contract over the seasons

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:35.220
and breathing gently, they absorb
the sea air and natural forest scents.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.430
During that time,
the craftsmen would try to imagine...

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:42.760
just what kind of aroma and what
kind of flavour would emerge.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.980
Their hearts trembled with fear and hope.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:51.230
When this barrel is opened,
we may have already left this world.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:55.650
But someone will open the barrel
and taste it for us all the same.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:57.700
That may have been

00:17:58.280 --> 00:17:59.860
a prayer.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:01.320
A prayer.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:02.740
A prayer.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:05.160
A world without war,

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:09.960
a peaceful world that can truly
enjoy whisky will one day come.

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.670
They must have strongly believed
such a world would come.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.760
And it was not only whisky.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:22.100
Also Eau-de-Vie, which was originally
a brandy made from distilled wine.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:26.020
The distillation method
was taken to Scotland

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:29.890
and became Scotch whisky,
"Uisge Beatha."

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:32.980
In Northern Europe,
Akvavit was born.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:35.400
In Russia, it was called Vodka.

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:40.240
The word "Vodka" itself coming from
the Russian phrase "Zhiznennia Voda".

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:42.990
Eau-de-Vie, Uisge beatha,

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:45.990
Akvavit, Zhiznennia Voda...

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.000
They all mean one thing,

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:50.960
Water of life.

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:53.000
Water of life.

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.380
Water of life.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:19:01.300
No matter the bottle, we bartenders
accept the spirits as the water of life,

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:04.010
passing these on to our customers
who are our life.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:06.260
With a prayer for life,

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:08.810
that connects us to life.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.100
For example, the Rob Roy.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:12.900
A drink which is named

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:17.280
after the nickname for
Robert McGregor, a gentleman thief

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:21.570
who gave money to the poor,
in 17th Century Scotland.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:26.450
Gentleman thieves give money...
Then what do bartenders give?

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:30.620
You know by now, don't you...?

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:37.460
What it is that you can keep giving
your customers as a bartender.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.170
So please always keep the answer in mind.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:44.930
I will.

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.100
Eden hall.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:59.320
It would appear that he, too, is about
to take a short nap to heal up.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:06.320
While dreaming of lost travellers who
will one day return here to share a glass.

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:10.080
The name of that cocktail is
the Glass of the Gods.

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:14.250
The last drink that can save
those souls hurt by solitude,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:15.960
that have nowhere to go.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:22.010
That is the story of
Eden hall's curious beginnings.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.220
People love spirits.

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:30.970
But I wish to love those
who love spirits, too.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:35.560
We bartenders have a formless job.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:40.980
Which is precisely why we must uphold
the highest ideals, or be doomed to fail.

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:44.030
Even if we somehow fake
our way through our job,

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.660
we cannot fake our way through life.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:52.910
So I feel fortunate
to have found the job of bartender.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:56.500
Not because I have made it my profession,

00:20:57.130 --> 00:21:00.420
but because I have made it my way of life.

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:13.520
